  "id": "Compound::DB01595 - Gene::2566",
  "verbalization": "Nitrazepam is likely to interact with Gabrg2 through a shared mechanism involving GABA receptor activity. The evidence suggests that Nitrazepam binds to Gabra4 or Gabra2, which in turn participate in benzodiazepine receptor activity, ultimately leading to Gabrg2 being involved in this process. The varying binding targets (Gabra4 vs. Gabra2) indicate that Nitrazepam may interact with different GABA receptor subunits, which could explain the diverse effects observed in different contexts. The presence of Gabrg2 as a participant in the benzodiazepine receptor activity pathway suggests that Nitrazepam may modulate GABA receptor function, potentially through a common downstream effect. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for Nitrazepam in influencing GABA receptor activity, which could contribute to its pharmacological effects.",
